{"text": "There are several ways. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Designers can minimize the likelihood of a controls accidental activation. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Perhaps the easiest way is to make sure. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The control is far away. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> So that the operator is unlikely. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> To come into accidental contact with it. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> We could also increase the control resistance. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Which in turn will increase the force. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Required for the operator to activate the control. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> We might recess the control into the control panel. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Or place it behind a barrier. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> If the control is not used frequently. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> We can place a protective cover over it. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Or lock the control into position. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> These options not only make the control harder to operate. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But they create a sequence of several actions. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That the operator must take to activate the control. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> If operation requires more than a single step. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The operator must go through a sequence. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Not only of actions. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But also of decisions to make each action. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Each point in the activation process. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Then provides an opportunity. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> For the operator to reconsider his or her choice. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> To activate the control.", "alignment": {"characters": ["T", "h", "e", "r", "e", " ", "a", "r", "e", " ", "s", "e", "v", "e", "r", "a", "l", " ", "w", "a", "y", "s", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "D", "e", "s", "i", "g", "n", "e", "r", "s", " ", "c", "a", "n", " ", "m", "i", "n", "i", "m", "i", "z", "e", " ", "t", "h"